CAT = "cpafarPublic"; SEG = "PUB"
Q = [
("A registrant files a Form 8-K with the SEC to report:",
 "Material events between periodic reports", ["Annual audited financial statements", "Quarterly unaudited statements", "Proxy matters for the annual meeting"],
 "Form 8-K is the current report for specified material events, such as an acquisition or a change in auditors. Annual reports go on Form 10-K and quarterly reports on Form 10-Q."),
("Item 7 in Part II of Form 10-K contains:",
 "Management's discussion and analysis", ["Financial statements and supplementary data", "Disclosures about market risk", "Executive compensation tables"],
 "Part II Item 7 is MD&A. Item 7A covers quantitative and qualitative disclosures about market risk, and Item 8 holds the financial statements and supplementary data."),
("Item 8 in Part II of Form 10-K contains:",
 "Financial statements and supplementary data", ["Management's discussion and analysis", "Disclosures about market risk", "A list of the company's properties"],
 "Item 8 holds the audited financial statements and supplementary data. MD&A is Item 7 and market risk disclosures are Item 7A."),
("The financial statements in Part I of a Form 10-Q are:",
 "Unaudited, reviewed by the auditor", ["Audited by the independent auditor", "Unaudited, with no auditor review", "Audited for the fourth quarter only"],
 "Quarterly statements in a 10-Q are unaudited, but Regulation S-X requires an independent accountant to review them before filing. There is no 10-Q for the fourth quarter."),
("A large accelerated filer must file its annual report on Form 10-K within:",
 "60 days after fiscal year-end", ["75 days after fiscal year-end", "90 days after fiscal year-end", "45 days after fiscal year-end"],
 "Form 10-K is due 60 days after year-end for large accelerated filers, 75 days for accelerated filers and 90 days for all others."),
("A Form 8-K for most reportable events must be filed within:",
 "Four business days", ["Ten business days", "Two business days", "Fifteen calendar days"],
 "Most 8-K items are due within four business days after the event occurs."),
("Net income is $500,000. Cumulative preferred dividends of $40,000 were declared this year and 100,000 common shares were outstanding all year. Basic earnings per share is:",
 "$4.60", ["$5.00", "$4.20", "$5.40"],
 "Basic EPS is income available to common shareholders over weighted shares: ($500,000 - $40,000) / 100,000 = $4.60. Ignoring the preferred dividends gives $5.00."),
("A company had 120,000 common shares on January 1, issued 40,000 on April 1 and bought back 12,000 as treasury stock on October 1. Weighted average shares for the calendar year are:",
 "147,000", ["148,000", "150,000", "160,000"],
 "120,000 + 40,000 x 9/12 - 12,000 x 3/12 = 120,000 + 30,000 - 3,000 = 147,000. 148,000 is the year-end count, which ignores timing."),
("A company issues a 10% stock dividend in September. For weighted average shares, the new shares are:",
 "Restated as of the earliest period", ["Weighted from the issue date", "Ignored in the denominator", "Added to diluted shares only"],
 "Stock dividends and splits bring in no resources, so the shares are treated as outstanding from the start of the earliest period presented, and prior EPS is restated."),
("Employees hold 10,000 options with an exercise price of $30. The average market price for the year is $40. Under the treasury stock method, incremental shares for diluted EPS are:",
 "2,500", ["7,500", "10,000", "3,333"],
 "Exercise brings in $300,000, which buys back $300,000 / $40 = 7,500 shares, so 10,000 - 7,500 = 2,500 incremental shares. 7,500 is the buyback, not the net increase."),
("Stock options whose exercise price is above the average market price for the period are:",
 "Excluded from diluted EPS", ["Included at the full share count", "Included in basic EPS", "Added at half weight"],
 "Such options would raise EPS if exercised, so they are antidilutive and excluded. Options never enter basic EPS."),
("Net income is $900,000 and 200,000 common shares are outstanding all year. Bonds convertible into 50,000 shares carried interest expense of $60,000, and the tax rate is 25%. Diluted EPS is:",
 "$3.78", ["$3.84", "$3.60", "$4.50"],
 "If-converted: add back after-tax interest of $60,000 x 75% = $45,000, giving $945,000 / 250,000 shares = $3.78. Adding back the full $60,000 gives $3.84."),
("Under the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock, the numerator of diluted EPS:",
 "Adds back the preferred dividends", ["Adds back interest, net of tax", "Is left unchanged by the conversion", "Adds back common dividends"],
 "If the preferred shares had converted, no preferred dividends would have been paid, so the dividends deducted for basic EPS are added back. Interest add-backs apply to convertible debt."),
("A small company prepares its statements on the cash basis. Its statement of results is best titled:",
 "Statement of revenue collected and expenses paid", ["Statement of income and retained earnings", "Statement of operations and net income", "Statement of comprehensive income"],
 "Statements under a special purpose framework use titles that show they are not GAAP statements. Titles like income statement or statement of operations imply accrual-based GAAP reporting."),
("A cash-basis company collected $640,000 from customers. Accounts receivable rose from $70,000 to $95,000 during the year. Accrual-basis revenue is:",
 "$665,000", ["$615,000", "$640,000", "$735,000"],
 "Revenue = cash collected + increase in receivables: $640,000 + $25,000 = $665,000. Subtracting the increase gives $615,000."),
("A company paid $300,000 in salaries. Accrued salaries were $12,000 at the start of the year and $8,000 at the end. Accrual-basis salary expense is:",
 "$296,000", ["$304,000", "$300,000", "$288,000"],
 "Expense = cash paid - beginning accrual + ending accrual: $300,000 - $12,000 + $8,000 = $296,000. Part of the cash paid settled last year's accrual."),
("Which change from pure cash basis is a common modification in modified cash basis statements?",
 "Capitalizing and depreciating fixed assets", ["Accruing all receivables and payables", "Recognizing deferred income taxes", "Estimating expected credit losses"],
 "A modified cash basis adds a few accrual items with substantial support, such as capitalizing long-lived assets and depreciating them. Accruing everything would make it the accrual basis."),
("Cash is $60,000, marketable securities $40,000, accounts receivable $140,000, inventory $180,000 and prepaid expenses $20,000. Current liabilities are $300,000. The quick ratio is:",
 "0.80", ["1.47", "1.40", "0.33"],
 "Quick assets are cash, marketable securities and receivables: $240,000 / $300,000 = 0.80. Including inventory and prepaids gives the current ratio of 1.47."),
("Cost of goods sold is $720,000. Inventory was $160,000 at the start of the year and $200,000 at the end. Inventory turnover is:",
 "4.0 times", ["3.6 times", "4.5 times", "5.0 times"],
 "Turnover = COGS / average inventory = $720,000 / $180,000 = 4.0. Dividing by ending inventory alone gives 3.6."),
("Net income is $300,000, income tax expense $100,000 and interest expense $80,000. Times interest earned is:",
 "6.0 times", ["5.0 times", "3.75 times", "7.0 times"],
 "Earnings before interest and taxes are $300,000 + $100,000 + $80,000 = $480,000, and $480,000 / $80,000 = 6.0. Leaving out the interest gives 5.0."),
("Net income is $180,000. Stockholders' equity was $1,100,000 at the start of the year and $1,300,000 at the end. Return on average equity is:",
 "15.0%", ["13.8%", "16.4%", "7.5%"],
 "Average equity is $1,200,000, and $180,000 / $1,200,000 = 15.0%. Using ending equity gives 13.8% and beginning equity 16.4%."),
("Net income is $250,000, interest expense $40,000, income tax expense $70,000, depreciation $60,000 and amortization $20,000. EBITDA is:",
 "$440,000", ["$420,000", "$360,000", "$400,000"],
 "EBITDA adds back interest, taxes, depreciation and amortization: $250,000 + $40,000 + $70,000 + $60,000 + $20,000 = $440,000. Leaving out amortization gives $420,000."),
("Average accounts payable are $90,000 and cost of goods sold is $730,000. Using a 365-day year, days payable outstanding is about:",
 "45 days", ["41 days", "60 days", "8 days"],
 "Daily COGS is $730,000 / 365 = $2,000, and $90,000 / $2,000 = 45 days. 8.1 is the payables turnover, not the days."),
("Budgeted revenue was $500,000 and actual revenue was $470,000. The revenue variance is:",
 "$30,000 unfavorable", ["$30,000 favorable", "$15,000 unfavorable", "$60,000 unfavorable"],
 "Actual revenue fell short of budget by $30,000. Lower revenue than planned is an unfavorable variance."),
]
